<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Troubles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1359">1359</a>–<a href="/years/1381">1381</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Great Turmoil · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Troubles were a period of civil war in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> from 1359 to 1381, after the murder of Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berdibeg">Berdi Beg</a>. Over twenty khans rose and fell, while the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> ruled the west through puppet khans. The chaos loosened the Horde's grip on the Rus' principalities, letting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> defeat Mamai at Kulikovo in 1380, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> reunited the khanate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-troubles.jpg" alt="Great Troubles" width="159" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tibicena · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> had reached its height under Özbeg Khan, who reigned from 1313 to 1341. In the following decades several Mongol khanates fell, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> reached the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, in the 1340s, killing many in the khan's army and up to 25% of the Rus' population. After Özbeg's death a series of royal murders began: his son Tini Beg was killed by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg">Jani Beg</a> in 1342, and Jani Beg possibly by his own son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berdibeg">Berdi Beg</a> in 1357. Berdi Beg's murder by his brother Qulpa in 1359 is usually taken as the start of the Troubles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Qulpa's two sons were Christians named Michael and Ivan, which outraged the Muslim population, and his brother Nawruz Beg soon killed him and them. With the death of Nawruz the line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a>, founder of the Horde, died out, and rival branches of the Jochid clan fought for the throne: no fewer than 24 khans were enthroned, and in one year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> was taken by 6 different pretenders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>, who was not a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and so could not claim the throne, ruled through puppet khans such as Abdullah and Muhammad Bolaq.</p><p>Neighbouring powers exploited the strife. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogdan-i-of-moldavia">Bogdan I of Moldavia</a>, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>'s forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blue-waters">Battle of Blue Waters</a> and took the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, and rival khans granted rival patents to the Rus' princes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a> of Muscovy beat Mamai's general Begich at the Vozha River on 11 August 1378 and Mamai himself at Kulikovo in 1380. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>, nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urus-khan">Urus Khan</a>, had meanwhile won the backing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> and taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, and his victory over Mamai at the Kalka River in 1381 made him undisputed khan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 1382 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>, allied with the princes of Tver, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a>, besieged and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a> had fled north, confirming that the Rus' princes remained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Horde. The historian Charles J. Halperin argues that Moscow gained more from the later war between Tokhtamysh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> than from Kulikovo, and describes the Horde as having declined during these decades from a major European and Asian power to a minor regional one.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Troubles” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army's weakness showed during the <b>Great Troubles</b>: in 1380 the warlord Mamai was defeated by Grand Prince Dmitry of Moscow at the Battle of Kulikovo, although Khan Tokhtamysh sacked Moscow two years later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Military</a></li></ul></section>
